finish('worst-case-np-hardness-implies-average-case-hardness',
 id='TCS-0012',title='Does P≠NP imply a samplable NP problem outside AvgP?',
 area='Average-case complexity',criterion='reductions',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Is the implication $\mathrm{P}\ne\mathrm{NP}\Longrightarrow(\mathrm{NP},\mathrm{PSamp})\nsubseteq\mathrm{AvgP}$ true? That is, assuming P≠NP, must there exist a language L∈NP and a polynomial-time samplable distribution ensemble D={Dₙ} such that no deterministic exact decider for L runs in average polynomial time under D, in the moment-based sense defined below?''',
 definitions=r'''A sampler is one randomized Turing machine S which, given 1ⁿ, halts within q(n) steps on every random tape for a fixed polynomial q. Its output distribution Dₙ is over finite binary strings x; |x|≤q(n), and n is the ensemble parameter, not necessarily |x|. Sampling is exact with independent unbiased coin flips, and S has no advice or oracle. An exact decider A receives (1ⁿ,x), halts, and returns the membership bit of L correctly for every n and x. Write T_A(1ⁿ,x) for its running time. The pair (L,D) belongs to AvgP if there exist such an A, ε>0, C>0, and integer k≥1 with $\mathbb E_{x\sim D_n}[T_A(1^n,x)^\varepsilon]\le C(n+1)^k$ for every n≥1. The constants and A may depend on L and S but not on n. This moment definition is the robust Levin notion, not the requirement that the first moment itself be polynomial. The algorithm is deterministic and never gives a wrong answer; difficult inputs may take a long time. NP uses ordinary polynomially bounded certificates and deterministic polynomial-time verification. Both the hard language and its sampler must be fixed across all input lengths.''',
 answer_criterion='Prove or disprove the displayed implication for ordinary Turing computation. A positive answer must derive existence of a pair (L,D) outside the precisely defined AvgP from P≠NP alone. Hardness under an arbitrary uncomputable distribution, a conjecture about one particular random-instance model, or a reduction requiring additional hardness assumptions does not establish this implication. A relativized separation only limits relativizing proof methods.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Worst-case hardness says that an algorithm has difficult inputs. It does not say that a feasible instance generator produces them with enough probability to obstruct average efficiency. The sampler is therefore part of the mathematical problem. It may depend on the language, but it cannot contain an uncomputable table selecting a hard input at every length.','book'),
  paragraph('A padding example shows why a fixed familiar distribution is insufficient. Encode a formula x as 0 followed by |x|²−1 further zeros, a separator 1, and x. Satisfiability of these padded strings remains NP-complete under polynomial reductions. Under the uniform distribution on strings of a fixed length, valid encodings are so rare that checking the format and brute-forcing the rare valid formulas has polynomial average cost. An easy uniform distribution does not remove worst-case hardness.'),
  paragraph('Levin’s moment condition makes average efficiency stable under polynomial slowdowns. If a machine is simulated with its running time raised to a fixed power, a correspondingly smaller positive moment still suffices. This prevents the answer from depending on an incidental choice of machine representation. Exact computation with this runtime guarantee differs from a heuristic allowed to give incorrect answers on a small fraction of inputs.','survey'),
  paragraph('Distributional completeness is already available: Impagliazzo and Levin extend the theory to polynomial-time samplable ensembles. Completeness identifies a common average-case target; it does not prove that this target is hard from P≠NP.','survey'),
  paragraph('There are barriers for nonadaptive oracle reductions from worst-case NP hardness to average-case NP hardness: the relevant reductions would collapse the polynomial hierarchy. Such a barrier leaves other proof mechanisms open. Recent reductions for specific time-bounded Kolmogorov problems use additional assumptions or specific forms of hardness, so they do not supply the implication on this card.','barrier')],
 why='This asks whether worst-case intractability necessarily survives on inputs generated by a feasible process. It is the basic bridge between the standard theory of NP-completeness and distributional complexity, and a prerequisite issue in attempts to derive useful cryptographic hardness from P≠NP alone.',
 importance=dict(score=97,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='One of the central missing implications in complexity theory, connecting worst-case lower bounds to feasible instance generation and the limits of algorithms on typical inputs.'),
 progress=[
  step('1986–1990','Levin develops robust average-time definitions and distributional completeness; Impagliazzo and Levin extend completeness to samplable ensembles. The survey develops the moment definition and this completeness theory.','survey'),
  step('2003','Bogdanov and Trevisan show limitations of nonadaptive worst-case-to-average-case oracle reductions for NP. Their result is a conditional obstruction to a reduction method, not a disproof of the unrestricted implication.','barrier'),
  step('2018 / revised survey 2021','Wigderson’s Open Problem 4.5 and the revised Bogdanov–Trevisan survey explicitly ask whether P≠NP implies average-case NP hardness.','book'),
  step('2026-01','Liu and Pass obtain a worst-case-to-average-case reduction for an exact time-bounded Kolmogorov problem under an exponential circuit-hardness derandomization assumption. The extra hypothesis is stronger and more specific than P≠NP.','recent')],
 references=[
  ref('book','Mathematics and Computation','Avi Wigderson',2018,'https://www.math.ias.edu/files/mathandcomp.pdf','27 March 2018 draft; Section 4.4 and Open Problem 4.5, printed pages 56–57'),
  ref('survey','Average-Case Complexity','Andrej Bogdanov; Luca Trevisan',2021,'https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0606037v3','17 August 2021 revision of the 2006 survey; Section 1.1.6; Definitions 1 and 4 in Chapter 2; polynomial moment bound equivalent to the stated O(n) convention'),
  ref('barrier','On Worst-Case to Average-Case Reductions for NP Problems','Andrej Bogdanov; Luca Trevisan',2003,'https://lucatrevisan.github.io/pubs/BT03.pdf','FOCS 2003; abstract and nonadaptive self-correction result, yielding coNP⊆AM/poly and a collapse of PH'),
  ref('recent','One-Way Functions and Boundary Hardness of Randomized Time-Bounded Kolmogorov Complexity','Yanyi Liu; Rafael Pass',2026,'https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.97','ITCS 2026, Article 97; exact MINK^poly reduction retains the stated exponential circuit-hardness derandomization assumption')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The original question is explicitly open in Wigderson’s book and the revised average-case survey. Current primary literature was checked through 10 September 2026; no resolution from P≠NP alone was located. The card fixes deterministic exact AvgP and strict polynomial-time sampling, rather than conflating them with randomized heuristic hardness.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
